\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Automatic mechanism for temple door actuation designed by Heron of Alexandria (62~CE). When the altar fire is lit, the air confined within cavity (E) undergoes thermal expansion, increasing the pneumatic pressure inside the water-filled reservoir (H). This pressure forces water through pipe (L) into the suspended cauldron (M). The resulting weight displacement overcomes the counterweight (R), thereby opening the doors. Once the fire is extinguished, pressure drops, and the system reverts to its initial equilibrium state. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {herondoor}.}}{2}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:herondoor}{{1.1}{2}{Automatic mechanism for temple door actuation designed by Heron of Alexandria (62~CE). When the altar fire is lit, the air confined within cavity (E) undergoes thermal expansion, increasing the pneumatic pressure inside the water-filled reservoir (H). This pressure forces water through pipe (L) into the suspended cauldron (M). The resulting weight displacement overcomes the counterweight (R), thereby opening the doors. Once the fire is extinguished, pressure drops, and the system reverts to its initial equilibrium state. Adapted from \cite {herondoor}}{figure.caption.6}{}}
